# Real-World DevOps Connection

Day 32 connects directly to real-world containerized application architecture.

A typical application may have:

```text
                Application
                     |
                     | test network
                     |
              +------+------+
              |             |
              v             v
          App Container   MySQL
                             |
                             |
                             v
                       Persistent Volume
```

### If the application container is recreated:

The application container can be replaced without losing database data.

### If the database container is recreated:

The database can reconnect to the same persistent volume.

### If the database IP changes:

The application can still connect using:

```text
mysql-db:3306
```

because the application uses the container/service name rather than a hard-coded IP.

This demonstrates an important DevOps principle:

> **Separate application lifecycle from data lifecycle.**

---

# Troubleshooting Connection

If an application cannot connect to MySQL, a practical troubleshooting flow is:

```text
1. Check containers
       ↓
docker ps

2. Check networks
       ↓
docker network ls

3. Inspect network
       ↓
docker network inspect test

4. Verify both containers are connected
       ↓
first-cont / mysql-db

5. Test name resolution
       ↓
mysql-db

6. Verify MySQL port
       ↓
3306

7. Check MySQL logs
       ↓
docker logs mysql-db
```

This is useful in real DevOps troubleshooting because connectivity problems can occur due to:

* Container not running
* Wrong network
* Container not attached to the expected network
* Incorrect database hostname
* Incorrect database port
* MySQL startup failure
* Application configuration issue

